From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 00:34:27 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] plat: rpi5: pinctrl: add BCM2712 D0 stepping tables

plat/rpi/rpi5/bcm2712_pinctrl.c only carries the C0 register map, but the pin
controller is not register-compatible between the two steppings. The VideoCore
firmware auto-applies overlays/bcm2712d0.dtbo on D0 boards, and that overlay
rewrites the node to

    compatible = "brcm,bcm2712d0-pinctrl";
    reg = <0x7d504100 0x20>;      /* C0 is 0x30 */

Both the mux and the pad registers move with it. For the pins this platform has
to configure for the Wi-Fi SDIO and the Bluetooth UART:

    pin  C0 mux / pad        D0 mux / pad
    24   reg 3 / reg 9       reg 2 / reg 5
    30   reg 3 / reg 9       reg 2 / reg 5
    32   reg 4 / reg 9       reg 3 / reg 5
    35   reg 4 / reg 9       reg 3 / reg 6

With BIT_TO_REG(), C0 pad_reg 9 is offset 0x24 -- four bytes past the end of the
D0 block. Measured on a D0 Raspberry Pi 5 running Xen with the driver domain as
SCMI agent 0: an SCMI bias-pull-up request on gpio30..35 wedged the requesting
CPU every time, with no output at all, because release builds default to
CRASH_REPORTING=0 and the console belongs to the hypervisor; and the mux
requests silently landed on the wrong pins, since C0 mux_reg 3 (offset 0x0c) is
D0 reg 3 = pins 32..39. The same mis-indexing explains the asynchronous SError
that a read of 0x107d504124 takes from u-boot, and the one a guest takes if the
node is handed to it directly with the C0 compatible from the base DTB.

Add the D0 register map, pin list and function list, transcribed from the
Linux driver drivers/pinctrl/bcm/pinctrl-brcmstb-bcm2712.c, with a
function-group list over them, and select between the C0 and D0 sets with the
new RPI5_PINCTRL_D0 build flag (default 0, so existing behaviour is
unchanged). All uses now go through BCM2712_PIN_REGS / BCM2712_GPIO_PINS /
BCM2712_PIN_FUNCS / BCM2712_FUNCS_GROUPS.

Where the tables come from: Linux's
drivers/pinctrl/bcm/pinctrl-brcmstb-bcm2712.c (raspberrypi/linux, rpi-6.18.y),
which is SPDX GPL-2.0+ and carries "Copyright (C) 2024-2025 Ivan T. Ivanov,
Andrea della Porta", "Copyright (C) 2021-3 Raspberry Pi Ltd." and "Copyright
(C) 2012 Chris Boot, Simon Arlott, Stephen Warren"; this file is BSD-3-Clause.
The D0 register map, pin list and function list carry the Linux values and
function names; only the macro names differ, and the pins D0 does not have are
spelled out. The function-group list is an index over the pin list, as the C0
one is.

The tables stay indexed BY PIN NUMBER, which is the invariant the file already
relies on to use one id for both pinctrl_get_group_name() and
bcm2712_pfc_fsel_set(). D0 numbering has holes (pins 0, 5-9, 16, 17 do not
exist), so those are spelled out with a new GPIO_HOLE() that sets mux_bit 0 and
pad_bit REG_BIT_INVALID -- without it a missing initialiser would leave {0, 0}
behind and aim a pad write at MUX register 0.

platform.mk also turns CRASH_REPORTING on. On this platform that is not a
debug-only nicety: a faulting EL3 access otherwise shows up only as a guest CPU
that stops answering interrupts.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
--- a/plat/rpi/rpi5/bcm2712_pinctrl.c
+++ b/plat/rpi/rpi5/bcm2712_pinctrl.c
@@ -38,6 +38,18 @@
 #define EMMC_REGS(n, pr, pb) \
 	[n] = { 0, ((pr)*4)*8 + (pb)*2 }
 
+/*
+ * A pin that does not exist on this stepping. Needed for D0, whose pin numbering
+ * has holes: the tables below are indexed BY PIN NUMBER (that invariant is what
+ * lets pinctrl_get_group_name() and bcm2712_pfc_fsel_set() use the same id), so
+ * the holes have to be spelled out. mux_bit 0 makes fsel_get/set a no-op and
+ * REG_BIT_INVALID makes pull_config_get/set refuse, so a request for one of these
+ * touches no register at all -- rather than the {0,0} a missing initialiser would
+ * leave behind, which would aim a pad write at MUX register 0.
+ */
+#define GPIO_HOLE(n) \
+	[n] = { 0, REG_BIT_INVALID }
+
 static spinlock_t bcm2712_pfc_lock;
 
 struct bcm2712_pin_regs {
@@ -443,6 +455,203 @@
 	PIN(53, sc0, enet0_rgmii, ext_sc_clk, _, _, _, _, _),
 };
 
+/*
+ * ===================== BCM2712 D0 stepping =====================
+ *
+ * The pin controller is NOT register-compatible between C0 and D0, and this is not
+ * a detail: the VideoCore firmware auto-applies overlays/bcm2712d0.dtbo on D0
+ * boards, which rewrites the node to
+ *     compatible = "brcm,bcm2712d0-pinctrl"; reg = <0x7d504100 0x20>;
+ * (C0 is 0x30). With the C0 tables, pin 30's pad register is pad_reg 9 = offset
+ * 0x24 -- four bytes past the end of the D0 block. On this board that access is
+ * unrecoverable in EL3: TF-A release builds have CRASH_REPORTING=0, so the CPU
+ * spins silently forever, and from the non-secure side it surfaces as the
+ * "Asynchronous SError" that both u-boot (md.l 0x107d504124) and a DomD with the
+ * pinctrl node in its DT were seen to take.
+ *
+ * Measured on a D0 Raspberry Pi 5 (Xen + OP-TEE + SCMI agent 0 = the driver
+ * domain): with the C0 tables an SCMI bias-pull-up request on gpio30..35 wedged
+ * the requesting CPU every time; the mux requests landed on the wrong pins for the
+ * same reason (C0 mux_reg 3 = offset 0x0C is D0 reg 3 = pins 32..39).
+ *
+ * Values transcribed from Linux's drivers/pinctrl/bcm/pinctrl-brcmstb-bcm2712.c
+ * (bcm2712_d0_gpio_pin_regs / bcm2712_d0_gpio_pins / bcm2712_d0_gpio_pin_funcs);
+ * GPIO_REGS/EMMC_REGS are spelled as there, and PIN takes BRCMSTB_PIN's
+ * arguments, so the two can be diffed. For the pins this platform has to mux,
+ * the D0 map puts everything inside the 0x20 window:
+ *     gpio24..31  mux reg 2 -> 0x08      gpio32..35  mux reg 3 -> 0x0c
+ *     gpio24..32  pad reg 5 -> 0x14      gpio33..35  pad reg 6 -> 0x18
+ */
